= Module 3: Regional Disparities & National Population Policies

== 3.1 Regional Variations in Population Growth
A prominent spatial dimension of India's demographic transition is the pronounced regional divergence in growth trajectories between northern and southern states:
- *Southern Low-Growth Belt:* Kerala (4.9% — lowest decadal growth among all major states), Tamil Nadu (15.6%), Andhra Pradesh (11.0%), Karnataka (15.6%), and Goa (8.2%) have all attained below-replacement fertility rates ($"TFR" < 2.1$). Kerala's pioneering demographic transition is attributed to near-universal female literacy, delayed age at marriage, and high-density rural primary health centers.
- *Northern & Central High-Growth Belt (EAG States):* The Empowered Action Group (EAG) states maintain high natural growth rates: Bihar (25.4%), Uttar Pradesh (20.2%), Rajasthan (21.3%), Madhya Pradesh (20.3%), and Jharkhand (22.4%). These states possess high total fertility rates (TFR), widespread rural poverty, and lower female literacy.

#important-box(title: "Adolescent Demographics & The Demographic Dividend")[
  - *Adolescent Population Share:* According to Census 2011, adolescents (defined as individuals in the *10–19 age group*) constitute *20.9%* of India's total population (comprising 25.3 crore persons; 52.7% male and 47.3% female).
  - *Socio-Demographic Vulnerabilities:* High incidence of early marriage, female school dropout rates, adolescent pregnancy, high maternal mortality, and widespread iron-deficiency anemia among adolescent girls (exceeding 56%).
  - *Demographic Dividend:* The colossal youth cohort represents a powerful economic window of opportunity, provided high-quality education, healthcare, and employment skills are developed.
]

