= Module 6: Occupational Structure & High-Yield Synthesis Mindmap

== 6.1 Occupational Matrix and Economic Status
The occupational structure of a nation reflects the proportion of its working population engaged in various economic sectors:
- *Census Economic Classification of Workers (Criterion: 183 Days):*
  - *Main Workers:* Persons who work in economically productive activity for *at least 183 days* (or six months) in the year preceding enumeration. They constitute *77.8%* of India's total working populace.
  - *Marginal Workers:* Persons who work for *less than 183 days* (six months) during the year. They constitute *22.2%* of total workers, reflecting significant underemployment.
  - *Non-Workers:* Dependent persons who do not participate in any economically productive activity (students, homemakers, infants, elderly retirees), comprising *60.2%* of India's aggregate populace.
  - *Work Participation Rate:* The percentage of total workers (Main + Marginal) to the total population:
    $ "Work Participation Rate" = frac("Total Workers (Main + Marginal)", "Total Population") times 100 = 39.8% $

