== 6.2 Sectoral Shift, Regional Work Participation & Gender Dynamics
- *Historical Sectoral Transition:* In 1951, the primary agricultural sector absorbed *74.6%* of India's workforce. By 2011, this declined to *54.6%* (Cultivators 24.6% + Agricultural Labourers 30.0%). Conversely, the proportion of "Other Workers" (secondary and tertiary sectors) surged from 25.4% to *41.6%*, reflecting industrial diversification and economic modernization.
- *Spatial Variations in Work Participation:*
  - *High Participation States:* Tribal and mountainous states exhibit significantly higher work participation rates: *Himachal Pradesh (51.9%)*, *Nagaland (49.2%)*, *Sikkim (50.5%)*, and *Chhattisgarh (47.7%)*. In these subsistence agrarian and hill economies, widespread female participation in manual terrace farming and livestock grazing inflates the overall work rate.
  - *Low Participation States:* Densely populated Gangetic plain states record lower participation rates: *Uttar Pradesh (32.9%)*, *Bihar (33.4%)*, and *Punjab (35.7%)*, driven by low female work participation and high educational enrollment.
- *Gender Disparity:* Male work participation stands at *53.3%*, whereas female work participation is restricted to *25.5%*. A vast proportion of female labor in household chores, cooking, and unpaid family farm assistance remains unrecorded in formal census accounting.

